# Template for flash/flash.cfg — the 56800E Flash Programmer's (fflash.exe)
# configuration file. Confirmed from the "56800E Flash Programmer User's
# Guide" (Rev. 0, 09/2005): fflash takes a config file rather than
# command-line flags for target device and debug interface selection.
#
# This board's on-board debug interface is OSJTAG (via USB port J8) —
# confirmed from NXP's "Getting Started with MCUXpresso SDK for
# MC56F83000-EVK" guide. An external P&E U-MultiLink is also supported as
# an alternative. Don't hand-write this file from scratch: CodeWarrior
# ships a real, per-device flash config in its own install tree — copy
# {CodeWarrior install}\MCU\M56800x Support\56800E flash programmer\
# MC56F837xx.cfg (yes, despite the "837xx" name — check its `mcu` line,
# it targets `mc56f83789` directly). This is CONFIRMED WORKING: verified
# by actually flashing and running hello_world on real MC56F83000-EVK
# hardware with this exact file, output read back over the OSJTAG serial
# bridge (see docs/SETUP.md step 6).
#
# What's confirmed from the fflash manual so far:
#   - fflash defaults to "the current configured device on the CCS server"
#     unless overridden
#   - -USB forces the USB interface (this board's OSJTAG path)
#   - -LPT<n> / -p<PORT> select a legacy parallel-port Command Converter —
#     not applicable to this board
#   - the device string is set via `mcu mc56f83789` inside the .cfg file,
#     not a command-line flag
# What's NOT yet confirmed (verify against your installed manual, usually
# under {CodeWarrior install}\Help\, before relying on it):
#   - whether there's a dedicated run/reset-after-program flag, or whether
#     "run after flash" is a setting inside the .cfg file itself
# Known gotcha, not a config problem: FFLASH.exe's CLI has a real bug
# where a failed connection exits silently (code 1, zero output, nothing
# logged) — see docs/SETUP.md step 6 for the diagnosis path (use the GUI
# 56800E Flash Programmer.exe to see the actual error instead).
